WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.890
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:12.370
Today we are going to see if a virtual AI
character can learn, or perhaps even invent

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.360
these amazing signature moves.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.550
And this is a paper that was written by Sebastian
Starke and his colleagues.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:26.520
He is a recurring scientist on this series,
for instance, earlier he wrote this magnificent

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.000
paper about Dribbling AI characters.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Look.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.129
The key challenge here was that we were given
only 3 hours of unstructured motion capture

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:38.129
data.

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:44.420
That is next to nothing, and from this next
to nothing, it not only learned these motions

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:50.859
really well, but it could weave them together,
even when a specific movement combination

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:53.640
was not present in this training data.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:01:00.999
But, as these motions are created by human
animators, and may show at least three problems.

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:07.310
One, the training data may contain poses that
don’t quite adhere to the physics of a real

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:08.790
human character.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:15.910
Two, it is possible that the upper body does
something that makes sense, the lower body

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:21.620
also does something that makes sense, but
the whole thing, put together, does not make

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.460
too much sense anymore.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:29.950
Or, three, we may have these foot sliding
artifacts that you see here.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:35.060
These are more common than you might first
think, here is an example of it from a previous

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:44.420
work, and, look, nearly all of the previous
methods struggle with it.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.370
Now, this new work uses 20 hours of unstructured
training data.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:56.250
Now, remember, the previous one only used
3, so we rightfully expect that by using more

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.800
information, it can also learn more.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:05.130
But the previous work was already amazing,
so, what more can we really expect this new

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.450
one to do?

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:12.220
Well, it can not only learn these motions,
weave together these motions like previous

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:19.280
works, but, hold on to your papers, because
it can now also come up with novel moves as

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.410
well!

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.549
Wow.

00:02:21.549 --> 00:02:27.950
This includes new attacking sequences, and
combining already existing attacks with novel

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.879
footwork patterns.

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:33.239
And it does all this spectacularly well.

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:40.069
For instance, if we show it how to have its
guard up, and how to throw a punch, what will

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:42.040
it learn?

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.889
Get this, it will keep its guard up while
throwing that punch.

00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:53.420
And it not only does that in a realistic,
fluid movement pattern, but it also found

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.420
out about something that has strategic value.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:07.409
Same with evading an attack with some head
movement and counterattacking.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:08.609
Loving it.

00:03:08.609 --> 00:03:11.450
But how easy is it to use this?

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:17.299
Do we need to be an AI scientist to be able
to invoke these amazing motions?

00:03:17.299 --> 00:03:23.799
Well, if you have been holding on to your
papers so far, now, squeeze that paper, and

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:24.909
look here.

00:03:24.909 --> 00:03:25.909
Wow.

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:29.730
You don’t need to be an AI scientist to
play with this!

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:30.730
Not at all!

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:35.010
All you need is a controller to invoke these
beautiful motions.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.769
And all this runs in real time.

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:39.680
My goodness!

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.969
For instance, you can crouch down and evade
a potential attack by controlling the right

00:03:44.969 --> 00:03:48.370
stick, and launch a punch in the meantime.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:54.459
Remember, not only both halves have to make
sense separately, the body motion has to make

00:03:54.459 --> 00:03:56.639
sense as a whole.

00:03:56.639 --> 00:04:00.250
And it really does, look at that.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:06.529
And here comes the best part, you can even
assemble your own signature attacks, for instance,

00:04:06.529 --> 00:04:14.180
perform that surprise spinning backfist, an
amazing spin kick, and, yes!

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.370
You can even go full Karate Kid with that
crane kick.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:26.520
And as a cherry on top, the characters can
also react to the strikes, clinch, or even

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.910
try a takedown.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:34.870
So, with that, there we go, we are again,
one step closer to having access to super

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:41.379
realistic motion techniques for virtual characters,
and all we need for this is a controller.

00:04:41.379 --> 00:04:45.810
And remember, all this already runs in real
time!

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.220
Another amazing SIGGRAPH paper from Sebastian
Starke.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:57.220
And get this, he is currently a 4th year PhD
student and already made profound contributions

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.810
to the industry.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:15.500
Huge congratulations on this amazing achievement.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:53.170
What a time 
to be alive!

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.999
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

